    {
      "type": "done",
      "answer": "HBM is not necessarily the best choice for every system because different memory technologies optimize for different constraints, and the ideal choice depends on the specific workload and system requirements.\n\nAccording to the lecture, the trade-offs are as follows:\n*   **DDR** favors capacity, expandability, and cost per GB.\n*   **GDDR** balances pin bandwidth and board cost.\n*   **HBM** favors bandwidth per package and per watt.\n\nBecause HBM is optimized specifically for high bandwidth per package and power efficiency, it may not be the optimal choice if a system prioritizes raw capacity, expandability, or lower cost per GB (where DDR would be better), or if the balance between pin bandwidth and board cost is more critical (where GDDR might be preferred). The lecture explicitly states that \"the choice depends on the workload and system constraints\" [S1][S6].\n\n**Source:** Page 59 [S1][S6].",
      "citations": [
        {
          "document_id": "ad66f2c8360da43ea0981bffa549956185949044062a0abe4689381ced423402",
          "chunk_id": "ad66f2c8360da43ea0981bffa549956185949044062a0abe4689381ced423402-p59-1",
          "page": 59,
          "text": "GPU Memory Hierarchy\n\nDDR, GDDR, and HBM Optimize Different Constraints\n\n\n\n\n\n▪ DDR favors capacity, expandability, and cost per GB. GDDR balances pin\n  bandwidth and board cost.\n▪ HBM favors bandwidth per package and per watt. The choice depends on the\n  workload and system constraints.\n\nMemory Wall     CPU Memory    GPU Memory    SOTA Memory     Advanced AI Computing and Systems        59",
          "preview_url": "/api/documents/ad66f2c8360da43ea0981bffa549956185949044062a0abe4689381ced423402/pages/59/preview",
          "source": "S1"
        },
